EFET welcomes this opportunity for feedback on the EMIR Review/Clearing Strategy and proposes the following recommendations:

Extend the list of eligible collateral. CCP should extend to a wider range the non-cash collateral demanded, not only for clearing members, but also for their clients (e.g., letters of credit, EUAs). EFET members do not dispose of same access to money market as clearing members. Additionally, that would free up more resources for investment.

Increase transparency and accessibility of initial margin models and calculation tools. central counterparties (CCPs) should make margin models and calculation tools more transparent and accessible to the clients of the clearing members, in order to increase, at all levels, the ability to predict and plan for market stress scenarios and resulting impact on margin requirements. This can be implemented by:
•	Better accessibility to well explained risk models and margin calculation tools of CCPs;
•	Educational efforts by CCPs on margin practices in normal and stressed market conditions, specific means used to respond to extremely stressed markets, addition of concentration and/or supplementary margins;
•	Consultation of market participants on risk model design and review, as their deep expertise on commodity risk management models could be better employed;
•	Increase visibility of margin positions held by CCPs;
•	Standardized term sheet about CCPs rules and conditions would help to increase readability of the main characteristics of CCP models. This would also ease the comparison between CCPs when it comes to identical products;
•	CCP risk model design and review on a recurrent basis.

Increase predictability of margin calls. Market participants (MPs) need real-time “as-is” margin calculation tools to verify short term liquidity demand. Additionally, MPs need to be able to run “what-if” scenarios, in order to be prepared for further extreme markets conditions and their impact on liquidity planning. This can be implemented by:
•	User friendly and standardized simulations tools offered by CCPs. Margin simulators are important tools for the preparedness of market participants. Clearing members and their clients need access to real-time calculations of initial and variation margin that include parameters such as planning, including liquidity planning; historical analysis; capabilities to respond timely to margin calls; “what-if” scenarios, in order to allow better mid-term liquidity planning. 
•	Provide application program interfaces (APIs) to avoid high cost for accessing margin calculators, at times, prohibitive for medium sized and smaller clients. 
•	Increase ability for clients to replicate margin calculations, including intraday margin call projections.

Higher responsiveness to market conditions to mitigate the impact of volatility on liquidity demand. CCPs have specific rules under EMIR to update margin parameters depending on market conditions. EFET argues that CCPs should have more flexibility to adjust models regarding projection on volatility during stressed market conditions to mitigate impact on margin requirements and liquidity demand. This can be implemented by:
•	Review API tools to reduce impact of market stress on liquidity management;
•	Enhance collaboration between CCP, clearing members and clients to help clients (ultimate payers) to better forecast and manage liquidity requirements.
•	The pass-through of intraday calls from clearing members to clients, due to high variable margin movements within a business day, should be reviewed.
•	Reflection if there can be better use and calibration of default fund contribution.

Finally, EFET strongly recommends a substantial increase of the EMIR clearing threshold for commodities to an internationally comparable and more systemic relevant level, necessary to facilitate the energy transition, enhance European competitiveness and improve market functioning, whilst safeguarding transparent and safe markets.

The non-ferrous metals industry recognises the need to take soil protection, the sustainable functions and use of the soil and restoration of soils to a balanced level within the EU.
We are committed to continuously improve our production performance to prevent, control, reduce and eliminate pollution as far as possible to protect human health and the environment. We support the process regulated under the New Soil Strategy. As a sector, we are actively engaged at all levels, political, and with the Member States, to improve soil health through improved science and better monitoring.
We welcome the opportunity to comment on the call for evidence for an impact assessment on the Soil Health legislation. Below are our key recommendations to ensure the science robustness and transparency of this exercise.
Key recommendations:
The EU Soil Health law should: 
- Ensure healthy and resilient soils capable of providing their crucial ecosystem functions and services; 
- Be based on sound scientific knowledge and a risk-based approach for sustainable soil use; 
- Be based on cost-effectiveness and cost-benefit analysis; 
- Consider the differences in local conditions (soil type, geogenic background concentrations, climate…); 
- Consider the bioavailability of metals in soil and sediments as it is a key factor to ensure an effective and ecologically relevant approach in risk evaluation and risk management of soil pollution and contamination; 
- Consider the other legislations in place/coming to tackle other environmental challenges (such as climate change); 
- Use available data and expertise (e.g. on effects, risks, SEA) gathered in the framework of other European legislations (e.g. REACH, Fertiliser Product Regulation); 
- Contribute to a better dissemination of the existing knowledge on the state of soils in Europe; 
- Use publicly available data or anonymise those data that are not, to guarantee transparency in this legislative process; 
- Ensure stakeholders involvement through expert working groups, workshops, consultations, …

Overall, we continue to emphasise the non-ferrous metals industry’s commitment to achieving a good ecological and chemical status of EU soils and to keep on improving the assessment of risk posed by metals in soils through the most updated scientific knowledge and methods.

Contribution of the Service for rural policies and information systems in agriculture of the Autonomous Region of Friuli Venezia Giulia

To better address the problems highlighted in framework A and to facilitate the achievement of the objectives of framework B of the Call for evidence for an impact assessment, as far as agriculture is concerned, it is considered essential that the legislative initiative provides:

1)	support and funds to improve essential knowledge at local level in terms of:
-	soil survey and mapping;
-	land use and land cover mapping;
-	mapping and monitoring soil threats: erosion, organic carbon decline, compaction, contamination, salinization, desertification, etc.;
-	mapping and monitoring soil biodiversity, including the microbiome, given its role in soil fertility.

If clear progress has been made in understanding and analyzing of the phenomena mentioned at european level, in recent decades budget restrictions of local and scientific entities/agencies have severely slowed down, if not prevented, mapping activities of the same at large scale (e.g. 1:25,000).
It is essential that these activities become eligible to EU support and funds, within its initiatives and policies, like Horizon, Life, etc.; synergies can be sought with the Common Agricultural Policy and in particular with tools such as IACS and AKIS, in order to involve farms in mapping and monitoring these phenomena at the farm scale.
It is important to identify indicators and parameters to standardize the collection and management of information and data in the countries of the EU.

2)	improvement of governance at horizontal and vertical level, and the existing planning tools (rural development, urban planning, and land and environmental management) in order to:
-	prevent further reductions in UAA as a result of legal and de facto land consumption (e.g., agricultural photovoltaics on the ground or elevated), to ensure food security in terms of quality and quantity;
-	rationally manage the land and agricultural soils, concentrating intensive production activities where assets are favourable for competitive agricultural activity, in any case based on sustainability; conversion to extensive forms of farming and traditional practices in unfavourable terrains - which in alternative require high inputs of water, nutrients and energy - would improve soil health, biodiversity and the recovery of historic rural landscapes; traditional and extensive agricultural practices need to be supported to overcome the competitive disadvantage;
-	restoring and closing nutrient and carbon cycles as much as possible on a local scale.

3)	limitation of the bureaucratic burden resulting from the possible introduction of additional procedures (e.g. health certificate, passport for excavated soil) by digitalization and use of information/data derived from the monitoring and planning activities carried out by the responsible bodies.

We welcome the Commissions initiative to propose a Soil Health Law in order to provide a comprehensive EU legal framework for soil protection, comparable to the level of water resource protection. As Austria draws its drinking water 100% from ground water resources, Austrian drinking water suppliers depend largely on well protected resources.
A proper soil health law supports the proper functioning of soil ecosystems and therefore protecting water quality and quantity. 
We particularly support: 
•	adequate integration and coordination of soil and water management;
•	the development of indicators for soil health and groundwater and a strict range of values ; 
•	monitoring and reporting on the condition of soil and groundwater;
•	measures that can contribute to reducing nutrient losses by at least 50% without deterioration in soil fertility (resulting in the reduction of fertiliser use by at least 20%) at the same time reducing potential contamination with hazardous substances; 
•	requirements for the sustainable use of soil so that its capacity to deliver ecosystem services is not hampered;
•	options to identify, register and remediate contaminated sites, and to apply the polluter pays principle;
•	reporting on the progress in managing soil contamination and;
•	options for a passport for excavated soil; 
•	long-term land use regulation to secure sufficient space for groundwater protection and drinking water safeguard zones.
Soil as a reservoir for groundwater
We would like to emphasize the important role soils play in water purification and infiltration. The Soil Health Law should include concrete objectives aimed at protecting drinking water resources by improving groundwater quality and quantity, and thereby contribute to reaching the goals of the Water Framework Directive (WFD). This necessitates among others, reducing agricultural emissions from manure/fertilizers and pesticides as well as remediating contaminated sites that have a serious negative impact on drinking water resources. Therefore, the effectiveness of the Soil Health Law and its target achievements should also be determined by the effects on groundwater quality and on the protection of drinking water supply. This includes adequate monitoring of groundwater quality and identifying polluting substances for the drinking water supply. From a quantitative aspect, soils – especially humus - play a vital role in storing waters, preventing surface run-off and reducing floods.  
Healthy soils to protect water
Soils play a vital role for water management. A healthy soil with sufficient organic matter has a buffer function for the water system, increases the filter capacity for groundwater, reducing the sensitivity to drought and flooding; thereby contributing to climate change resilience. Wetlands, peatlands and rural or urban nature-based solutions help storing and infiltrating rainwater locally. This prevents long distance surface water run-off and improves groundwater recharge rates. The Soil Health Law should enshrine the interdependence between water and soil. Hence, the Soil Health Law should incorporate the simultaneous monitoring of soil and groundwater quality, mainstreaming water quality monitoring objectives. Coupled with supporting the continuous development of knowledge on the positive role healthy soils play in further reducing diffuse pollution of groundwater. 
Although sewage sludge contains valuable nutrients, sludge must undergo a thorough analysis and risk assessment before applying it on agricultural areas to avoid unintentional contamination with PFAS, pharmaceuticals or other substances of emerging concern. 
*The Austrian Association for Gas and Water (ÖVGW) represents the interests of Austrian drinking water suppliers. Its members supply around 80% of Austria’s population with drinking water. 100 % of Austrian drinking water comes from groundwater.

Soil is the thin porous and biologically active medium that represents the upper layer of the earth's crust, consisting of mineral and organic components, water, air and living organisms. It represents the interface between earth, air and water and hosts a large part of the biosphere; capable of supporting plant life, it is characterised by its own flora and fauna and a particular water economy. It is divided into horizons with their own physical, chemical and biological characteristics.
Together with air and water, soil is essential for the existence of living species on the planet and performs a range of functions that place it at the centre of environmental balances. Despite this, it is too often perceived only as a support for agricultural production and as a physical base on which to develop human activities. It plays a key role in protecting groundwater, controlling the amount of atmospheric CO2, regulating surface water flows with direct consequences on flooding and landslides, maintaining biodiversity, nutrient cycles, etc. The health of the soil depends on its ability to function as a source of nutrients. Plant biomass depends on the health of the soil, with obvious repercussions on the entire food chain. As an extraordinarily diverse biological laboratory, the soil can be regarded as a complex, constantly evolving and in some respects still poorly understood living organism that provides mankind with the elements necessary to sustain itself. 
Soil is a common good, a non-renewable resource, a source of food, a filter and store for water and carbon in the atmosphere, a guardian of biodiversity and historical memory, a fundamental element of Italian landscapes, and a guardian for the prevention and mitigation of hydrogeological instability. 
Soil is a complex ecosystem that removal, sealing or degradation inexorably destroy; it takes over 500 years for 2.5 cm of fertile soil to be recreated. Consumed soil is lost soil, unavailable to us and future generations.
Soil is at risk worldwide.
Fertile soils on a global scale are a finite resource. If we look at the planet as a whole, eliminating areas covered by ice, areas at excessive altitude, areas that are too cold or too hot, deserts, areas of poor accessibility, coniferous forests, salt marshes, wetlands and tropical areas, the fertile land available for global agricultural function amounts to only 13-18% of all emerged land. 
This surface area is finite - because soil is a finite resource, so with the expected increase in world population over the next few years, the fertile soil per capita is set to decrease dramatically, with serious consequences for food production, water supply, loss of biodiversity, and climate change. 
We have now been farming a stable area of land for 20 to 30 years - 1.6 billion hectares of land - which cannot easily be expanded, especially if we want to farm sustainably. This means that as the population grows, the area available per capita will decrease. Each of us currently has about 0.3 hectares - about 2000 square metres - of productive agricultural land at our disposal; this means that everyone will have to adapt to living with less available fertile soil. According to FAO data, soil provides 95% of global food. 
In addition to the scarcity of fertile soil on the planet, there is also the phenomenon of soil consumption or degradation - soil sealing - which erodes part of these areas. 
Soil protection necessarily involves the legal recognition of soil functions of common interest. We need to protect al least the following soil functions:
1. biomass production (including agriculture and forestry); 
2. filtration, purification and storage of water; 
3. biodiversity (habitat, species and genus); 
4. physical platform for life and human activities; 
5. source of raw materials; 
6. carbon reservoir and storage; 
7. custody of cultural heritage.

Bodem, ondergrond en (grond)water zijn belangrijke onderdelen van de fysieke leefomgeving. Samen leveren zij een verscheidenheid aan diensten en producten aan de maatschappij zoals voedsel, biodiversiteit, delfstoffen, drinkwater, energie en opslagruimte voor (duurzame en fossiele) energie. De bodem, ondergrond en grondwater zijn van vitaal belang. Om de diensten die bodem, grondwater en de ondergrond kunnen leveren ook voor toekomstige generaties te behouden, is het van belang om het maatschappelijk gebruik van bodem en ondergrond in balans te brengen met de draagkracht en het herstelvermogen van het bodem-/watersysteem. 

Vanuit Noord-Nederland wordt de urgentie en ambities om te komen tot duurzaam gebruik en beheer van de bodem en ondergrond in een aantal relevante documenten beschreven.
* Strategie Bodem & Ondergrond (Provincie Drenthe 2021); 
* Startnotitie bodembeleid (Provincie Fryslân 2022);
* Meerjarenprogramma Ondergrond en Bodem 2020-2025 (Provincie Groningen 2020).

Advies aan de Europese Commissie: Neem kennis van bovenstaande documenten. Daarnaast attenderen wij u op de het advies van de RLI " De bodem bereikt " m.b.t. urgentie landbouw-, bos-, en natuurbodems. 


Binnen de 3 noordelijke provincies is veel regionale kennis m.b.t. het bodem-/watersysteem en hoe te komen tot duurzaam gebruik en beheer van de bodem en ondergrond. Een aantal aandachtspunten die wij aan de Europese Commissie willen meegeven zijn: 
•	Het bodem-/watersysteem maakt onderdeel uit/ is betrokken bij vele maatschappelijke opgaven, zoals de landbouwtransitie, energietransitie, herstel biodiversiteit en de klimaatopgave.   Het uitspreken/erkennen van huidige structuren en werkwijzen die niet bijdragen aan duurzaam gebruik van de bodem is gewenst. Geef duidelijkheid en richting in de beoogde wet en in de toelichting in de gewenste Afbouw en de gewenste Opbouw.  Enkele voorbeelden:
o	Afbouw:
	Gebruik van niet duurzame hulpstoffen (kunstmest en gewasbeschermingsmiddelen);
	Vervuiling van de bodem door gebruik van zeer zorgwekkende stoffen (ZZS) & depositie van verzurende stoffen;
	Injectie van drijfmest;
	Eenzijdig bouwplannen en monoculturen in de landbouw;
o	Opbouw:
	Recycling van grondstoffen (Stijgende vraag naar primaire grond- en delfstoffen met als gevolg meer ontgrondingen); 
	Teelt van biogrondstoffen;
	Bovengrondse en ondergrondse biodiversiteit bevorderen in alle opgaven (bouw, natuur, infra, landbouw etc.); 
	Behoud van het aardkundig Erfgoed;
•	Naast de beoogde wet ter bevordering van de bodemgezondheid is inzet op communicatie/participatie en stimulatie wenselijk;
•	Betrek partijen vroegtijdig in het proces, Dit verzoek voor input is (nog) niet binnen relevante sectoren zoals landbouw, natuur en bouw onder de aandacht;    
•	Draag zorg voor duidelijkheid in bevoegdheden.  Binnen het bodem-/watersysteem zijn diverse bevoegde gezagen actief. Geef ruimte in wetgeving om in bepaalde gebieden te kunnen prioriteren in bodemgebruik (bodembeschermingsgebieden).  

Advies aan de Europese Commissie: Neem kennis van de aanpak en gebruik de regionale kennis bij de totstandkoming van de wet ter bevordering van de bodemgezondheid.

Tot slot: de 3 noordelijke provincies geven met verschillende programma’s en beleidslijnen zelf ook vorm aan een ‘andere’ landbouw. Met de Regio Deal Natuurinclusieve Landbouw Noord-Nederland willen wij deze inspanningen een extra impuls geven. Kern van de Regio Deal is de gebiedsgerichte aanpak die ertoe leidt dat meer boeren straks een handelingsperspectief hebben om meer met de natuur te boeren. De gebiedsgerichte aanpak versterkt een ontwikkeling die nu vooral ‘van onderop’ op kleine schaal vorm krijgt; het samen en in samenhang zorgen voor bijvoorbeeld betere bodemkwaliteit.

Advies aan de Europese Commissie: stimuleer duurzame vormen van landbouw zoals natuurinclusieve landbouw (naast biologische landbouw) in de Farm-to-Forkstrategie. Dit draagt bij aan gezonde bodems.

The Slovak Soil Science Society (Societas pedologica slovaca) appreciates the adoption of the new Soil Strategy 2030 in 2021 (COM (2021) 699 final) as part of the European Green Agreement, as well as the proposals for a soil health law, which aims to have soils in good health by 2050 and make soil protection, sustainable use and restoration as the norm. The issue is in line with the 8 objectives of the EU Mission: A Soil Deal for Europe.

1) Definition of soil health
Soil health was defined in the Mission "A Soil Deal for Europe" as the continued soil capacity to support ecosystem services). We agree with this definition, we primarily support two groups of ecosystem´s services: provision and regulation services as the key ones. Other services (cultural and supporting) will depend on the possibility of obtaining available databases. In this respect, further research will be needed to address environmental factors more accurately and in detail, as well as impact of the applied agricultural and forestry management.

2) Soil health indicators and their range of values
For each country, it is a group of specific indicators reflecting the climate, type and soil texture and its use. A unique framework of indicators needs to be developed and established for the concept and development of soil health policy in Europe. E.g. the reference values of the indicators should represent a combination, or the sum of individual indicators according to the availability of databases. 

3) Requirements for sustainable land use
These requirements are to be supported by the definition of a set of sustainable land management practices, i.e. which enable the provision of soil-related ecosystem services. 
•	crop and cultivation systems: diversified crop rotation, cover crops and catch crops, legumes on grasslands, temporary forests, perennial crops, incorporation of crop residues;
•	consolidation of agricultural land due to the possible degradation effect of external influences;
•	tillage and cover: non-inverse / reduced tillage, no tillage, direct sowing;
•	nutrient management and crop protection: use of organic and mineral fertilizers suitable for the site and production system, efficient fertilization, use of soil treatments and bio-fertilizers; reduction of pesticide use;
•	water management: apply appropriate drainage systems, efficient irrigation, improve accumulation water capacity;
•	alternative practices (rotational legumes, precise – digitized farming practices, timing and location of mineral and organic fertilizers) and nutrient losses (cover crops and catch crops) can be used to reduce fertilizer use;
•	appropriate agroforestry systems have the potential to improve the condition of agricultural land in terms of multiple risks and undesirable conditions. 
•	for forest soils, sustainable land use is an integral part of sustainable forest use, so specific requirements will be in line with the EU's New Forest Strategy 2030 (COM (2021) 572 final) as well as the EU Biodiversity Strategy 2030 (COM (2020) 380 final). 
•	Sustainable management should be integrated and linked to a validated database of possible degradation impacts. It should comply with CAP ecosystems and other international regulations and restrictions concerning soil health. 

4) Monitoring and reporting on soil status
The Slovak Republic contributes to the European database with a relatively rich soil data database (ESDAC JRC, EU Soil Observatory - EUSO). Participates in activities for analysis, synthesis and harmonization of databases (INSPIRE Directive). Each Member State should:
•	create a network of land data owners / administrators, including the implementation of the INSPIRE Directive;
•	create a system of monitoring and interpretation of monitoring results in the unified reporting system;
•	link the results of the monitoring to the political and legal context, i.e. the issue of penalties for damage to soil health, subsidies for improving and maintaining soil health.

Both the chemical and agrochemical industries, as energy- and resource-intensive sectors, feel a special obligation to protect the environment ("Responsible Care"). For agriculture, soil is the basis of all production, therefore an efficient and sustainable soil management is essential. Besides that, intensive and efficiency-oriented land management in the past as well as the long tradition of use of many sites with consequences of world wars and "disproportionate" use from today's perspective have led to harmful soil changes, i.e., in particular chemical pollution. The enforcement of the German soil protection law and the various obligations arising from the technical regulations on preventive soil protection law (Ordinance on Installations for the Handling of Substances Hazardous to Water, etc.) have led to soil pollution being avoided or, if it has occurred, to its immediate restoration. National legislation thus already addresses soil protection intensively, which means that double regulation is to be avoided (subsidiarity principle). For example, predicted environmental concentrations of plant protection products in the soil are already determined and considered in the strict risk assessment within the framework of plant protection product authorisation. 

In order to be able to achieve the set targets of the EU biodiversity strategy and to protect EU soils, the following aspects should be taken into account.

Regarding the chemical industry (aftercare principle):

- A risk-based approach in connection with the respective soil use should be set in the assessment and derivation of measures for contaminated soils;
- The mere presence of contaminants in soil does not justify its classification as “contaminated”. For this, at least background values need to be considered and a rigorous risk assessment needs to be performed.;
- When assessing soil contamination, the use of the site should be considered. So-called "brown fields", i.e., previously or currently used sites, should be considered differently from use on new sites ("green fields"); For example, for industrial production sites there are already soil monitoring requirements in the Industrial Emissions Directive (Art 16) and a clear demarcation would be needed in the new soil law proposal
- There should be no binding soil and/or groundwater investigations for (planned) land transactions. Private agreements and all informations between contracting parties require by all means a special protection of confidentiality;
- "Safeguarding of contaminated sites" and "remediation of contaminated sites" should have equal priority; 
- Clear distinction between "remediation" and "restoration" should be provided;
- Bureaucratic effort and benefits should be weighed up regarding “passports for excavated soil”. For industrial sites, we recommend creating a "relocation clause" at the site under consideration of the “deterioration principle”;
- Biodiversity in soil is a very complex system that needs to be better understood in order to define biodiversity protection measures;
- Stakeholder participation in drawing up the EU priority list for pollutants in soils as well as in the definition of soil health indicators and their value range are strongly advised.

Regarding the agrochemical industry (precautionary principle):
- Already existing strict plant protection product authorisation in the EU should be recognised and proven soil risk assessments of plant protection products (i.e. sorption, degradation, accumulation, persistence, toxicity for soil organisms) should be considered;
- There is a need for promotion of innovative crop protection and fertiliser technologies (digitalisation/smart farming like “spot application”) to achieve the goals of the European Green Deal and its initiatives;

We would like to use this feedback on soil health as an opportunity to enter an in-depth exchange with the European Commission.

We are at your disposal at any time to answer your detailed questions.

Dear honorable Commission,

NABU welcomes the renewed efforts to protect and restore our European soils. We thank the Commission for the opportunity to take part in this consultation.

Restoring healthy soils is a multi-benefit strategy of largely unparalleled potential. 

Restoring the health of our soils can synergistically contribute to all of the 17 Sustainable Development Goals of the United Nations (Lal 2020).

We stress that the Soil Health Law is urgently needed 

-	for climate change mitigation and adaptation of European agriculture;
-	to preserve below- and aboveground biodiversity; 
-	to restore ecosystem services;
-	to catalyze the agroecological transformation of the European food system;
-	to make European food security resilient and;
-	to decrease indirect land use and land use change impacts of the European food system. 

Please find attached a more detailed paper which explains why actions should be taken and how they should best be operationalized.

Thank you most kindly.


(Lal, R. (2020). Soil science beyond COVID-19. Journal of Soil and Water Conservation April 2020, jswc.2020.0408A; DOI: https://doi.org/10.2489/jswc.2020.0408A)

Largely agreeing with the diagnosis provided with the initiative, UIRR would hereby express its preference for Option 2 with the following annotations:

1. Option 1 has been the path pursued for the past decade, and it has clearly failed.  Member State (+sector)-driven initiatives did not deliver the desired results.  The regulatory framework provided by the European Union has clearly been insufficient to produce the required solutions.

2. Option 3, while seemingly efficient and attractive, is not thought to be realistic.  We do not believe that Member States and national infrastructure managers would be willing to sign over the amount of power and competence to the envisioned European-level entities.

3. This leaves Option 3, which should include as a minimum the following:

(i) a European categorisation of train path types for both passenger and freight trains, complemented by a hierarchy for such train paths;

(ii) a European register for train-path owners, which would designate the entity taking the commercial risk for a train (the authorised applicant) to be the owner of the train path needed to enable the train to run; 

(iii) the use-it or loose-it principle in rail: in case an applicant would be found to have cancelled train paths or not run trains on train paths in excess of 3% of train paths reserved by the applicant for a calendar year period, the applicant should be restricted to reserving not more than 97% of the train paths of the previous period during the following year;

(iv) the declaration of socio-economic-cost-benefit analysis to serve as the basis for capacity allocation, where the actual contents and method of the socio-economic cost-benefit analysis should be placed into an EU Implementing Regulation;

(v) a single European, or regional capacity allocation bodies, to be put in charge of all cross-border train paths, overseen by a European organisation (agency) with regulatory capacities;

(vi) a single European train numbering (identification) system, whereby the same trainset is identified as a single train throughout the entirety of its journey.

UIRR will provide its input throughout the public consultation process to help create the regulatory framework that will deliver more cross-border freight trains in Europe.

DIGITALEUROPE thanks the Commission for the opportunity to provide feedback on the European Chips Act package. Our organisation represents multiple stakeholders across the semiconductor ecosystem, including in areas like chip design, chip production and chip usage.

As the proposal for a Regulation on the Chips Act moves forward, the co-legislators should:

a) Clarify the terms and conditions for the development and third-party access to the virtual design platform and the pilot lines in Pillar 1;

b) Increase legal certainty in Pillar 2 by adding details on eligibility criteria for European first-of-a-kind semiconductor facilities and by foreseeing, in exceptional circumstances, ‘’continued operating support” for the facility;

c) Designate a single, EU central entity for reporting requirements, rather than 27 different bodies, and remove confidential data from the scope of ‘’crisis stage reporting obligations’’ in Pillar 3;

d) Boost the involvement of industry in the European Semiconductor Board and give them full membership rights of the Board’s sub-groups.

We appreciate the opportunity to work closely with the EU institutions to ensure the most favourable business environment for semiconductor companies and the broader EU industrial base. In attachment, we include our position paper addressing key aspects in the overall package and offering more detail on concrete recommendations on the proposal for a Regulation.

•	IAB France represents the broad digital advertising and marketing ecosystem in France whose 150+ members include advertisers, agencies, publishers, and technology companies.

•	IAB France and its members support the general objectives of the Data Act to promote a competitive European market of data and to clarify the rules of access and use of data to enable a transparent and fair allocation of value between all actors in the market.  

•	We would like here to thank the European Commission for giving us the opportunity to share our feedbacks on the Data Act, on three main items specifically:

o	Clarifying the scope of the Data Act proposal as well as some its definitions;
o	Providing further guidance on the articulation with the existing European legal framework;
o	Reconsidering the interdiction of profiling set up by the Data Act proposal.

Volkswagen Group welcomes the efforts of the European Commission to establish a flourishing data economy within the EU. The Data Act constitutes a main pillar of the European Data Strategy and is an essential milestone to achieve the single market for data. 

We further welcome the commitment of the European Commission that the Data Act should establish a “level playing field” (Fair, Reasonable and Non-Discriminatory principles for data access – FRAND) among all stakeholders of the data economy and place the users of products and related services at the center. 

Please find attached VW Group’s extensive contribution to this consultation, and below a short summary of our position paper. 

Reviewing the draft Data Act from the perspective of a key player in the automotive industry where data is also a critical ingredient for further development and innovation, we believe that several terms and provisions of the current draft need to be refined to provide more legal certainty for all stakeholders concerned.

In some key product characteristics vehicles are very different from many other products subject to the Data Act: A vehicle is a complex and integrated product with high standards concerning the security, safety and physical integrity of its direct users as well as of any other involved road users. The automotive industry more generally is characterized by multi-tiered supply-chain networks, sophisticated requirements to ensure the safety of passengers and vehicles, complex IP agreements between different stakeholders and an average product lifespan of 15-25 years. Therefore, some of the provisions of the Data Act which aim to uniformly regulate all types of “smart products” are only practicable to a limited degree for the automotive sector. Whilst we appreciate that the Data Act cannot individually address any and all sector-specific particularities, we hereinafter only focus on aspects which the Data Act does not sufficiently take into account and which should cause wider concerns even beyond the particularities of the automotive industry. 

The main goal of the Data Act is to promote data-driven innovation across all sectors by facilitating access and use of data for the benefit of individuals, businesses, public sector bodies and researchers. However, the draft Data Act as it stands has also got a potential to create obstacles for exactly these goals. Our concerns on the current draft of the Data Act are extensively elaborated in the attached position paper. Some of the main ones are, e.g.:

-The definition of the key term data needs to be refined and clearly delimited from data which is not subject to the Data Act’s rights and obligations (such as derived or inferred data and volatile data); 

-Access to in-vehicle data or real time access to data can only be granted where this is “relevant and appropriate” subject to a balancing of interests test; in particular, the holder must have a right to deny direct access to in-vehicle data where significant safety or security vulnerabilities are at stake;

-The crucial term competing product as used in Art. 4.4 and 6.2 lit e. should be defined explicitly to ensure the effectiveness of that provision; VW Group would clearly prefer to see the development of competing related services subject to the same restriction as competing products; 

-Similarly, other key terms such as user, data holder, data recipient and related service should be further refined, or at least additional interpretative guidance should be provided in the Recitals;

-…

While we are of the opinion that some provisions of the Data Act will need to be refined, we are convinced that data sharing as fostered by the Data Act will allow for mutual benefits and lead to a growth in innovation. Therefore, VW Group is determined to engage in constructive discussions and contribute to a high-quality legislative process.

La industria del neumático acoge con satisfacción la propuesta de Ley de Datos, ya que representa una regulación horizontal, de alto nivel y basada en principios que tiene como objetivo implementar un marco de gobernanza intersectorial para el acceso y uso de datos en línea con el espíritu de la Estrategia Europea de Datos. Una Ley de datos exitosa será esencial para la transformación del uso de datos en Europa en todos los sectores, incluido el desarrollo adicional de la movilidad inteligente y sostenible.
La Ley de datos establece una gran cantidad de principios que contribuirán a un mejor intercambio de datos en beneficio del usuario. Damos la bienvenida en particular a los siguientes principios, que instamos a los colegisladores a mantener como el núcleo de la Ley de datos:
•	la obligación de diseñar y fabricar productos para que los datos generados durante su uso sean accesibles al usuario, ya sea una empresa o un particular (artículo 3);
•	el principio de transparencia que obliga al fabricante a publicar los datos que probablemente se generarán por el uso del producto (artículo 3);
•	el derecho del usuario del producto a acceder y utilizar los datos generados por el uso del producto y los servicios relacionados (artículo 4);
•	el derecho del usuario del producto a ordenar el intercambio de datos generados a través del uso del producto con terceros elegidos por él y en base a los términos FRAND (artículo 5);
•	el requisito de interoperabilidad que respalda la estandarización de conjuntos de datos y otros elementos necesarios para compartir datos de manera efectiva (artículo 23).
También esperamos que los colegisladores mejoren aún más la propuesta de la Comisión en una serie de áreas en las que consideramos que hay margen para aumentar la claridad y fortalecer la intención de la Ley de datos. Estos incluyen claridad sobre el uso de datos derivados por parte de terceros elegidos por el usuario y el alcance de la protección contra términos de contacto injustos y garantías de compensación.
Como se describe en la exposición de motivos, la Ley de datos se complementará con una regulación sectorial en el sector de la movilidad para abordar los "desafíos específicos del sector, incluido el acceso a las funciones y recursos de los vehículos".
Esta futura legislación, que se propondrá a finales de 2022, es de vital importancia para garantizar el desarrollo y el crecimiento de toda la cadena de valor europea de los servicios de movilidad conectada. A tal fin, la regulación sectorial deberá abordar:
•	La extensión del alcance a las funciones y recursos del vehículo, incluida la interfaz hombre-máquina (HMI) del vehículo;
•	Medidas de transparencia para los proveedores de servicios
•	Estandarización del conjunto de datos común tal como está presente en la sesión del Grupo de Expertos en Ciberseguridad y Acceso a Datos de Vehículos en 2019;
•	Estandarización de criterios de seguridad y ciberseguridad para otorgar acceso a funciones y recursos de datos de vehículos;
•	Definición e implementación de derechos de acceso para terceros bajo el gobierno de una autoridad independiente.
Estos requisitos técnicos armonizados clave que faltan, también deben alinearse con el marco de la Ley de datos en virtud del cual el OEM, como titular de los datos en su función de fabricante de vehículos, puede aplicar las medidas de protección técnica adecuadas, incluidos los contratos inteligentes, para evitar el acceso no autorizado a los datos y para garantizar el cumplimiento. Sin embargo, consideramos fundamental que las medidas técnicas de protección permitidas por el artículo 11 de la Ley de Datos no se utilicen como medio para obstaculizar el derecho del usuario a proporcionar datos a terceros de manera efectiva.
La regulación específica del sector también brindará una oportunidad adicional para aportar claridad sobre cómo implementar los principios de la Ley de datos en el sector automotriz, incluidos los artículos 6 y 9.

IVC begrüßt den Schritt in Richtung "Eine Substanz - eine Bewertung" und unterstützt eine enge Zusammenarbeit zwischen EU-Agenturen und wissenschaftlichen Einrichtungen. Ein zweckmäßiger "Ein Stoff - eine Bewertung"-Ansatz sollte zu folgenden Ergebnissen führen.
- Erhöhte Effizienz und Vorhersehbarkeit;
- Verbesserte Konsistenz der Bewertungen und ihrer Ergebnisse unter Verwendung desselben Datensatzes;
- Verbesserte Robustheit der Bewertungen;
- Einbindung des richtigen Fachwissens am richtigen Ort zur richtigen Zeit;
- Bereitstellung von maßgeschneiderten Bewertungen im Rahmen spezifischer Rechtsvorschriften, sofern relevant;
- Optimale Nutzung der Ressourcen. 

Um dieses Ziel zu erreichen, sind Maßnahmen auf mehreren Ebenen erforderlich, darunter:
- Enge Koordinierung der Bewertungen zwischen den verschiedenen vorgelagerten Generaldirektionen, wissenschaftlichen Ausschüssen (einschließlich wissenschaftlicher Gremien) und Agenturen auf europäischer Ebene; dies schließt eine stärkere Koordinierung der Regulierungsinitiativen der Mitgliedstaaten ein;
- Gemeinsame Nutzung von Daten durch Ausschüsse und Agenturen;
- Anwendung einer einheitlichen Gefahrenbewertung;
- Zentralisierung der Instrumente und Methoden zur Expositionsbewertung auf einer gemeinsamen Plattform;
- Sicherstellung eines maßgeschneiderten Risikomanagements;
- Erhöhung der Transparenz von Entscheidungen und Prozessen.

Als IVC sind wir der Meinung, dass sowohl die Industrie als auch die Regulierungsbehörden von dem Ansatz "ein Stoff, eine Bewertung" profitieren werden, wenn er gut umgesetzt wird. Die Klarheit des Prozesses wird die Vorhersehbarkeit erhöhen und zur Rationalisierung der Ressourcen auf beiden Seiten beitragen.
Wir unterstützen das von Cefic für diese Konsultation vorgelegte Positionspapier.

CIRFS welcomes the move towards "One Substance - One Assessment" and supports close cooperation between EU agencies and scientific institutions. A fit-for-purpose "One Substance - One Assessment" approach should lead to the following outcomes.
- Increased efficiency and predictability;
- Improved consistency of assessments and their results using the same data set;
- Improved robustness of assessments;
- Involvement of the right expertise in the right place at the right time;
- Provision of tailored assessments within the context of specific legislation, where relevant;
- Optimal use of resources. 

Achieving this goal requires action at multiple levels, including:
- Close coordination of assessments between the different DGs upstream, scientific committees (including scientific panels), and agencies at the European level; this includes greater coordination of Member States' regulatory initiatives;
- Data sharing between committees and agencies;
- Application of a single hazard assessment;
- Centralizing exposure assessment tools and methodologies on a common platform;
- Ensuring tailored risk management;
- Increasing transparency of decisions and processes.

As CIRFS, we believe that both industry and regulators will benefit from the "one substance, one assessment" approach if it is implemented well. The clarity of the process will increase predictability and help streamline resources on both sides.
We support the position paper submitted by Cefic for this consultation.

CIRFS welcomes the move towards "One Substance - One Assessment" and supports close cooperation between EU agencies and scientific institutions. A fit-for-purpose "One Substance - One Assessment" approach should lead to the following outcomes.
- Increased efficiency and predictability;
- Improved consistency of assessments and their results using the same data set;
- Improved robustness of assessments;
- Involvement of the right expertise in the right place at the right time;
- Provision of tailored assessments within the context of specific legislation, where relevant;
- Optimal use of resources. 

Achieving this goal requires action at multiple levels, including:
- Close coordination of assessments between the different DGs upstream, scientific committees (including scientific panels), and agencies at the European level; this includes greater coordination of Member States' regulatory initiatives;
- Data sharing between committees and agencies;
- Application of a single hazard assessment;
- Centralizing exposure assessment tools and methodologies on a common platform;
- Ensuring tailored risk management;
- Increasing transparency of decisions and processes.

These different elements are explained in more detail below. As CIRFS, we believe that both industry and regulators will benefit from the "one substance, one assessment" approach if it is implemented well. The clarity of the process will increase predictability and help streamline resources on both sides.
We support the position paper submitted by Cefic for this consultation.

BSEF – the International Bromine Council - welcomes the opportunity to contribute to the call for evidence and welcomes this initiative to make the use of expertise and resources more efficient by proposing the reattribution of technical and scientific work on chemicals performed under the relevant pieces of legislation to European agencies (ECHA, EFSA, EMA), including work of the Scientific Committee on Health, Environmental and Emerging Risks (SCHEER) and Scientific Committee on Consumer Safety (SCCS). But also enlarge this to activities for which COM services are using external consultants.

In this context, it:
•	makes perfect sense that, independent of the area of application, the “basic evaluation of substances properties (hazard assessment)” is carried out starting from the same scientific basis that is established in a consistent manner;
•	shall however, not be lost out of sight that re-attribution of work should not lead to responsibilities moving to one Agency to avoid that the Agency or committee is not able to handle the workload and hence would jeopardise the quality of the work or results in the use of scientifically unsound decisions/approaches;
•	shall be avoided that overlapping evaluations are taking place in parallel within the same regulatory context. Risk management measure processes should not be started during an on-going substance evaluation, where data gaps may be identified and therefore pre-empting the step in the substance evaluations that identifies the most appropriate risk management measures to be identified or data to be gathered to be available;
•	shall be ensured that whatever body carries out the risk evaluation shall have available the necessary and adequate expertise within the application domain of the use of the chemicals that may be using different criteria for very valid reasons in context of the scope of application (e.g. food or medicines); 
•	shall be avoided to have duplication in the evaluation of risk management measures under different regulatory frameworks, including also COM services based initiatives with external support; 
•	shall be ensured that the natural sequential order to go from hazard evaluation  risk assessment  risk management shall be respected.

For example, substances which are already being addressed under REACH Substance Evaluation or risk management, measures should not be facing proposals for possible restrictions under other regulatory initiatives, such as RoHS.
•	Currently, TBBPA (2,2',6,6'-tetrabromo-4,4'-isopropylidenediphenol, EC no. 201-236-9), which has a complete REACH dossier, is undergoing a “Substance Evaluation under REACH”, in parallel a number of risk management processes have already been started: a harmonized classification (CLH) process is being discussed, (Brominated) Flame retardants are foreseen to be discussed in the REACH restriction road map under development by COM, and a proposal to identify TBPPA as substance of very high concern (SVHC) is being prepared by Norway. 
•	At the same time, RoHS has been assessing TBBPA in a parallel process, with a view on providing a dedicated restriction dossier for TBBPA, not making use of the existing specific hazard data under REACH for this substance, but following another hazard assessment approach based on unacceptable read across.
In the case of RoHS, as commented in our contribution to the call for evidence to the Impact Assessment, we note that ECHA have the experience, skills, process (including consistent risk based methodologies) and rigor to support evaluating substances and discuss possible restrictions, and should be involved more in overlapping processes (e.g., RoHS) to reduce redundancies.

Any outcome should not create additional administrative burden for industry, should not result in multiple processes to be run in parallel and clear legal certainty should be ensured.

The Japanese electric and electronic industrial associations, JEITA, CIAJ, JBMIA and JEMA, respect the accomplishment achieved by EU laws for the safety and environment. We appreciate the ongoing collaborative approach and are pleased to contribute to this assessment. 
We agree that the toxicity of the substance itself should be assessed by ECHA. However, the possible emission or exposure from the articles would be far lower than from the substance or mixture. The reason why is that the chemical composition of the articles should remain as it is during its useful life, as the special shape, surface or design of the articles determine their functions. Therefore, risk relating to the articles should be considered separately from the chemicals. 
Furthermore, to assess chemicals used in the complicated articles, such as EEE, socio-economic aspects are indispensable. It does not only relate to the chemical properties, but also needs technical and practical knowledge such as the possibility of actual substitution in the product and current market conditions, and the potential impact on recycling. 
If ECHA or any other agency has sufficient technical and practical knowledge, it can make a legitimate risk and socio-economic impact assessment. However, under any framework, it is essential to have not only knowledge about chemical substances but also technical and practical knowledge about final products in order to examine actions related to substances contained in EEE. 
Specifically, the person who assesses the chemicals contained in the complicated articles has to be able to cover the points of consideration that are different from REACH when considering the management of such substances. For example, the current conditions (Article 6(1)) for adding a substance to the list of restriction under RoHS Directive 2011/65/EU would be basically acceptable, provided that substances are properly assessed from the point of view of WEEE which is article and the best option is transparently chosen based on socio-economic impact assessment. In the assessment, following aspects are specifically investigated: 
(a) The substance could have a negative impact during EEE waste management operations, including on the possibilities for preparing for the reuse of waste EEE or for recycling of materials from waste EEE; 
(b) It could give rise, given its uses, to uncontrolled or diffuse release into the environment of the substance, or could give rise to hazardous residues, or transformation or degradation products through the preparation for reuse, recycling or other treatment of materials from waste EEE under current operational conditions; 
(c) It could lead to unacceptable exposure of workers involved in the waste EEE collection or treatment processes; 
(d) It could be replaced by substitutes or alternative technologies which have less negative impacts. 
We consider that the condition (d) should be most carefully assessed, because this condition makes the restrictions of substances actually used in the complicated articles, such as RoHS, feasible. 
In addition, when assessing the availability of alternative technology and the socio-economic impacts, the agency should take into account the following elements: 
- The specific service of the substance (how and why it is used?); 
- Reliability requirements to finished products; 
- Operational conditions (to take into account operations within harsh environment); 
- Average product lifetime; 
- Typical re-design cycle; 
- Re-qualification process and timing; 
- Technical and economic availability of substitutes; 
- Complexity of supply chains; 
-Interaction with other legislations on finished products, such as time needed for re-certification in relation to other EU safety legislation, such as LVD, EMC, ATEX, and International Standards such as IEC 61508 and 61511.

The European Women’s Lobby (EWL) recognises that this Directive proposal is a historic landmark and that it fills significant legislative gaps on violence against women and girls (VAWG) across the EU, especially on the legal definition of rape, the recognition of female genital mutilation as a criminal offence and the prosecution of key forms of online violence. It also seeks to bring harmonisation in penalties, limitation periods for the prosecution and decision on the covered forms of violence, access to justice and support services to women and girls throughout the EU.

However, the EWL also believes the proposal can be strengthened by:
•	seeking the inclusion of VAWG in the list of Eurocrimes as underlined in a recent appeal by the EWL and the European Parliament;
•	ensuring that all forms of violence are legislated upon;
•	tackling sexual exploitation of women and adopting the Equality Model.

The Forum Disuguaglianze Diversità (Forum DD) welcomes the Proposal for a Directive of the European Parliament and of the Council on Corporate Sustainability Due Diligence and APPRECIATES in particular:
1. the adoption of binding rules imposing specific duties extended along the entire value chain;
2. the introduction of duties for corporate directors to control risks concerning general and third-party interests, and in particular the linkage of their duties to the pursuit of the goal of sustainability per se (Art. 25), and not as means to maximize the value of financial investments;
3. the involvement of stakeholders in the most important steps of the process for the development of the due diligence policies at the company level;
4. the establishment of national independent supervisory authorities, free of conflicts of interest and with adequate powers;
5. the extension of due diligence obligations not only to EU companies, but also to non-EU companies generating a high turnover in the Union and to the their global value chain.
At the same time, the Forum DD notes a number of deficiencies, so that to correct them it RECOMMENDS:
a) the integration of the list of human rights and environmental standards relevant for due diligence obligations with a complete reference to the ILO Conventions, as a minimum;
b) the introduction of an explicit reference to the social dimension of sustainability, i.e. the fight against inequalities, when defining the directors’ duty of care (Art. 25) to “take into account the consequences of their decisions for sustainability matters”;
c) the statement that sustainability is not an external constraint on the company’s operations, but part of its purpose as it is one of the goals the company must pursue;
d) the application of the rules in force in the various states concerning the breach of directors' duties even when the liability action is brought by third parties (other than the company and the shareholders) and for damages that have harmed non-financial interests like those protected by the directive;
e) the overcoming of the fragmentation of stakeholder participation by providing for the systematic form of stakeholders representation and consultation, especially Unions and NGOs, at each step of the due diligence policy, and by avoiding discretion through the specification of appropriateness, fairness, relevance and good faith criteria for the identification of stakeholders and the conduct of dialogue;
f) the provision of the possibility for victims to request that the law applicable to violations occurring in value chains be the law of the state where the parent company is headquartered or where the event giving rise to the damage occurred.
g) the institution of supervisory authorities conceiving them as an opportunity for the development of economic democracy, with stable forms of stakeholder consultation, as well as communication channels easily activated by citizens to report violations.
In conclusion, the Forum DD urges the EU institutions to re-launch the legislative initiative on Sustainable Corporate Governance. Consistently with the declarations that corporate governance should be inspired by long-term visions and aimed at sustainability goals in the interest of all stakeholders, they should not be excluded from decision-making processes that affect their interests recognized as deserving protection.
Instead, it should be stated that the purpose of the company as a whole is to intentionally pursue, in a balanced, fair and efficient manner, the interests of all stakeholders involved in the value creation process and interested in preventing environmental impacts and reducing climate change.
As a consequence, corporate governance should provide that company workers, and those belonging to the value chain, as well as local representatives of environmental concerns, are given rights and powers of information, consultation and co-decision through which a fair balance can be achieved.

ANIA, the Italian Insurance Association, fully support the aim of promoting sustainable governance which represents a key issue for the sustainable transformation of the European economy.
Interventions should however focus on limited principles set in hard law (with a principle-based approach) to be combined with an extensive soft law regime, thus allowing each company to exploit its sustainable potential in the most appropriate and effective way.
ANIA recognises that the Corporate Sustainability Due Diligence (CSDD) proposal appears to be going in the right direction, even though there are some points of attention and some issues that should be better specified and calibrated.
In particular, ANIA deems of the utmost importance to properly address the following issues:
•	ensure consistency and alignment with European legislation on sustainable finance already in force or in the process of being finalised (Sustainable Finance Disclosure Regulation, Taxonomy Regulation, Corporate Sustainability Reporting Directive) and with the integration of sustainability already carried out in the insurance sectoral legislation (e.g. Solvency II and Insurance Distribution Directive) which already cover, at least in part, governance issues, with an approach that may differ from those proposed in the CSDD proposal;
•	require due diligence requirements to be applied only at consolidated level, as procedures, plans and targets are generally defined by the parent company, while applying to all the subsidiaries;
•	provide a more specific definition of the value chain, that for the insurance sector should be limited to the direct business partners only;
•	ensure a clear, proportionate and achievable enforcement system, which fully respects the specificities of the national legislations both in terms of civil liability and in terms of "directors' duty of care", avoiding to impose – even indirectly - on the Member States changes to the national systems;
•	ensure a supervision system based on national authorities, avoiding the establishment of an additional layer of supervision at the European level, with the risk of increasing only the system’s complexity, while there are already existing European supervisory authorities to promote coherence and cooperation.

IOM welcomes this initiative and is pleased to have the opportunity to provide feedback on the proposed Directive in the form of an attached paper. This paper outlines IOM’s recommendations to strengthen corporate sustainability due diligence mechanisms and supply chain protections. It highlights the essential role of migrant workers in companies’ value chains and the need to move beyond traditional due diligence approaches which may have limited effects for the protection of migrant workers. Accordingly, IOM recommends the new Directive to pay special attention to any particular human rights impacts on migrant workers who may be at heightened risk of vulnerability or marginalization.

Labour migration and migrant workers  are a key contemporary feature of global supply chains, with an estimated 164 million migrant workers globally representing around 5% of the global labour force. Migrant workers are present in most enterprises and workplaces integrated into the global economy through supply chains of various sectors. At the same time, migrant workers remain disproportionately vulnerable to exploitation and abuse, representing almost a quarter of the 25 million victims of forced labour globally. Unscrupulous business behaviour leaves migrant workers at increased risk of exploitation due to, among others, economic pressures, the high costs of migration (often due to inflated recruitment fees), discrimination, and a lack of legal protection and access to justice in countries of destination. 

The complexity and lack of visibility and transparency in supply chains is mirrored in the multiplicity of intermediaries who facilitate migration and the complexity of related labour migration processes which can impact the protection of migrant workers. Accordingly, the complexity of supply chains, its multiple tiers as well as vertical and horizontal business relationships within, challenge the effectiveness of current due diligence systems vis-à-vis migrant workers.

To ensure full respect for human and labour rights, IOM recommends due diligence being an integral part of a comprehensive and systemic approach that reflects the complexity of supply chains and various risks faced by migrant workers to strengthen corporate sustainability due diligence mechanisms and supply chain protections envisioned in the new directive. Therefore, IOM formulated the following key recommendations:

1)	Create comprehensive and robust corporate and supply chain policies to protect migrant workers;
2)	Analyse migrant worker recruitment and employment practices to create legitimate, effective and transparent remediation programmes; 
3)	Increase capacity-building and awareness-raising on migrant workers’ rights, ethical recruitment and international labour standards; 
4)	Mainstream the wellbeing of migrant workers and respect for their human and labour rights in companies’ business strategies; 
5)	Apply a multi-stakeholder approach to contribute to corporate duty of care and due diligence monitoring; 
6)	Recognise adverse impacts for migrant workers and workers in the informal economy and setting minimum standards for the protection of migrant workers;  
7)	Align the new Directive with existing international standards on labour and human rights;  
8)	Include and protect migrant workers’ rights in the new Directive.

Special considerations for migrant workers’ labour and human rights is vital to foster sustainable and responsible corporate behaviour throughout global value chains. IOM believes that a comprehensive approach to due diligence aligns to existing international standards on labour and human rights and is anchored in broader human rights considerations and programming to effectively protect migrant workers in complex supply chain contexts. IOM stands ready to support the EU, its Member States and the private sector in strengthening and systemising corporate sustainability due diligence, for a better protection of all migrant  workers.

AIM high-level preliminary comments on CSDD proposal:

AIM – the European Brands Association – is supportive of an EU harmonised legislative framework for human rights and environmental due diligence. We welcome the fact that the European Commission finally released its proposal for a Directive on Corporate Sustainability Due Diligence in late February 2022 but have a number of concerns on key aspects of the proposal.

We particularly recommend the following improvements:

•	adopt a maximum harmonisation approach to the due diligence obligations set out in the proposed Directive to avoid fragmentation of requirements between Member States’ national regimes, as well as to ensure legal certainty and a level playing field for companies operating across the EU single market;
•	reframe the scope of the due diligence obligations in the proposed Directive to allow companies to prioritise their efforts based on the severity of actual or potential adverse impacts; 
•	resolve the ambiguity of the civil liability provision in the proposed Directive, which is based on unclear definitions and sets a scope of liability that exposes companies to heightened legal risks for harms beyond their control, potentially leading them to focus more on managing the risk of litigation rather than carrying out robust due diligence;
•	ensure legal certainty by providing further clarity on the interaction between directors’ duty of care (Article 25) and responsibility for setting up and overseeing due diligence (Article 26), as well as giving flexibility to companies as to which level the directors’ duties shall apply;
•	define what constitutes a “severe human rights and environmental harm” in an interpretative guidance, following constructive dialogue amongst all relevant stakeholders, and taking into account already existing instruments or standards; 
•	align the content and format of the reporting with regards to due diligence in the proposed Directive with those required by the Corporate Sustainability Reporting Directive, and provide the possibility for companies to refer to global consolidated reporting at group level; 
•	set minimum requirements for natural and legal persons to be entitled to bring administrative or legal complaints based on a strict “direct interest in acting” standard with a view to preventing frivolous or abusive complaints and lawsuits; 
•	require the issuance of guidance for assessing the fitness of industry schemes and multi-stakeholder initiatives as part of a company’s overall due diligence approach with a view to ensuring effective due diligence;
•	require the issuance of detailed guidance regarding pro-active engagement with affected or potentially affected stakeholders at key moments in a company’s due diligence process clarifying that this complements reactive engagement with affected or potentially affected stakeholders through complaints procedures.

AIM will provide more detailed comments on the proposed CSDD Directive in due course.

OACPS response to EU consultation on the Directive on Corporate Sustainability Due Diligence

This Submission is made on behalf of the Members of the Organisation of African, Caribbean and Pacific States. The Organisation is composed of 79 Member States,  Africa(48), Pacific(15) and Caribbean(16) regions. The objective of the Organisation is to promote sustainable development and integration of its Members into the Global Economy. 70% of the Members of the OACPS are commodity-dependent and export most of their products to the European Market through the EPAs and the EBA Initiative. 

The OACPS is committed to the UN Sustainable Development Goals, including on its human rights and environment related objectives, and in so doing is supportive, in principle, of the EU’s Green Deal and the Farm to Fork Strategy. In one vein, the new proposed Directive on Corporate Sustainability Due Diligence presents opportunities while at the same time may present challenges as OACPS produce must track and in certain cases modify their production  and supply systems, to demonstrate that goods and products exported to the EU market, have not contributed to deforestation, or indeed child labour.  In this regard, the OACPS wants to emphasis on the following:

	In relation to Third countries- the Directive should take into account the special and vulnerable situation of OACPS Countries composed of Least Developed Countries (LDCs) and Small Island Developing States (SIDS) whose economies are dominated by small holder farmers and micro, small and medium enterprises (MSMEs);

	The new Directive should not become a disguised form of protectionism and result in insurmountable barriers to trade, thereby negatively impacting OACPS producers and exporters;
	 “Sustainability” must be human centred and thus include economic aspects giving priority to SDG 1 : No Poverty; Therefore, promoting instruments such as  the “Living Income Differential (LID)”developed by Cote d’Ivoire and Ghana in favour of their Cocoa Industry”;
	The Economic aspect is an indispensable step in human rights and environmental protection and promotion;
	The EU must ensure that this Directive does not result in a “cut and run” situation, whereby EU Companies abandon their suppliers from third countries facing human rights and environment challenges but instead promote enhanced collaboration between the two parties to address and solve the challenges identified;
	The Directive should facilitate meaningful dialogue with relevant stakeholders along the value chains including Farmers, to ensure working together in a participatory manner; and 
	Recognition should be given to the fact that countries are just recovering from the COVID 19 Pandemic and also currently being impacted negatively by the war in Ukraine.  

Specifically on Article 14.3 on Accompanying Measures (AM); 
	Clear guidelines on how the Accompanying measures can be accessed should be indicated
	Sufficient and adequate transition periods should be guaranteed to allow for adaptation
	Targeted technical and financial support should be channeled to OACPS Producers and Exporters as to allow them to comply with the new Directive; 
	Compliance costs should not be transferred to the small holder farmers; 
	AM should facilitate the setting up of transparency, traceability and monitoring systems in OACPS countries that will enable enforcement and compliance with the Directive;
	Facilitate the establishment of a Platform for information on best practices;
	Consideration of setting up a Green Aid for Trade facility; and 
	Complementary tailored financial solutions should be promoted.

In conclusion, the OACPS is committed and ready to work closely with its longstanding development partner the European Union to raise awareness and facilitate engagement with our Members on accompanying measures that could be adopted to address the concerns but also explore the opportunities that arise.

FESI welcomes the CSDDD proposal published by the European Commission. Based on learnings from decades of implementing human rights and environmental due diligence processes in members’ supply chains and a comprehensive review, FESI calls for the following amendments:
1. Establishment of level playing field and avoidance of market fragmentation;
As a globally operating industry, we ask for globally applicable standards, with a harmonised regulation across the EU as a minimum, and we strongly urge the Commission, the Parliament, and the Council to strive for a level playing field to avoid market fragmentation.

2. Guidance and clarification on the used definitions and required terminology;
The sporting goods industry is seeking further clarification on concepts such as “value chains”, “established business relationships”, “civil liability”, as well as the financial role of SMEs in an audit process and complaints procedure, which might complicate the understanding and implementation of the Directive.

3. Support for the inclusion of multi-stakeholders’ initiatives and alignment with internationally recognised instruments;
FESI fully endorses the acknowledgment of multi-stakeholder initiatives and industry schemes and their contribution to the identification, mitigation, and prevention of adverse impacts, which aligns with internationally recognised instruments on human rights due diligence of UN and OECD.

4. Scope: Control of complete value chains impossible;
The concept of “value chains” is too broad and would lead to uncontrollable obligations and cover unforeseen risks. The current Commission’s proposal could potentially divert the focus and resources of buyers from those activities and operations where risks have higher potential.

5. Concerns over the impact on the relationships with suppliers and partners;
The introduction of “established relationship” concept is unclear and has a potential for companies to distance themselves from long-lasting relationships. Suspending or terminating contracts based on potential impacts, may bring consequences to smaller suppliers.

6. Directors’ duties of care and the inclusion of environmental factors;
FESI questions the value of including the environmental factors and the Paris Agreement in Art. 15 and 25 as they are not aligned with existing OECD and UNDP due diligence tools, and it should be addressed by other EU-level initiatives.

7. Grievance mechanisms – ensure effectiveness and prevent from abuse with adequately addressed concept of prioritisation and severity;
Complaint mechanisms must be effective and must not contribute to abuse or undue complication and the possibilities of complaint. However, the open-ended concepts of “legitimate”, “well-founded” and “substantiated” in the proposal increase the risk for “unreasonable solicitation on companies”. Furthermore, the wording around the compensation of damages should be clarified.

8. Civil liability to be limited to one's own attributable actions;
Extensive civil liability entails boundless uncertainty for companies. Any form of liability should, in principle, be based on whether an actor has caused or contributed to the damage or is otherwise directly linked to it.

9. Review and reporting obligations: Overlapping of various reporting obligations threatens to overwhelm companies with over-bureaucratization;
Due to many different reporting obligations around sustainability, companies are increasingly charged with duplication of efforts to report. The proliferation of EU initiatives should aim at establishing a harmonisation that would combine coherent yet manageable reporting obligations.

FESI firmly believes that strong and ambitious EU legislation can make a tangible contribution to improving human rights along the value chain, helping simultaneously businesses of different sizes to become more resilient and future oriented. However, if such legislation is to be meaningful, factors raised in this paper should be considered.

UNICEF welcomes the initiative of the European Commission for a Directive introducing a sustainability due diligence requirement for companies as well as a monitoring and enforcement mechanism.  

To effectively prevent, address, and remedy harms to children, children’s rights must be explicitly reflected in both the text of the Directive and in all instruments supporting its implementation. Any deprivation suffered by a child because of negative business impacts can have life-long consequences and result in a lifetime of lost opportunities. Ensuring that children’s rights are fully realized is significant for sustainable development because it breaks the intergenerational transfer of deprivation and exclusion. All businesses impact on children’s rights in some way. While some progress has been made regarding child labour, business’ impact on children goes beyond it. However, companies rarely consider children as independent rights holders and stakeholders, thus impacts on children often remain invisible and therefore unaddressed.  

Because the experiences and perspectives of children differ from those of adults, mechanisms and public policies designed around adults do not capture salient issues for children. Consequently, it is imperative that the Directive integrates a stronger and more explicit child rights perspective. To do this, UNICEF’s headline recommendations are as follows: 

1) Explicitly recognize that children as well as other groups in vulnerable and marginalized situations require special attention in business’ due diligence and in accessing justice, through the introduction of a new article on “groups in vulnerable and marginalized situations.”  

2) Expand the scope of the due diligence obligation: to cover the entire value chains of business; to fully include the finance sector; and with a commitment to develop a clear roadmap for the progressive extension of the due diligence obligation to other businesses and industries.  

3) Include a stronger emphasis for child rights-based approaches to companies’ due diligence.  

4) Adopt a comprehensive package of supporting measures that include: implementation guidelines developed in consultation with key stakeholders; coherence among other legislative initiatives such as the EU Corporate Sustainability Reporting Directive and processes relating to the digital environment, including the Digital Services Act; EU’s foreign policy and international cooperation efforts to effectively support business, member States and third countries in strengthening business respect for child rights. 

Attached are UNICEF’s more detailed analysis and recommendations to strengthen the text of the Directive. In this policy brief we highlight UNICEF’s concerns and how to address them in the Directive. Specifically, we identify: 

a) the need to explicitly recognize children as independent and separate stakeholders to avoid the continued disregard of adverse impacts on children and the necessary amendments to the text (pp.2-5); 

b) the risks linked to the limited scope of the due diligence obligation for children and how to avoid them (pp. 5-7);  

c) the potential impact of introducing the concept of “established business relationships” that would leave out activities where children are significantly affected (pp. 7-8);  

d) the need to introduce a stronger child and human rights-based approach to due diligence and the risks of overly relying on contractual clauses and verification mechanisms for an effective prevention and remediation of harms to children (pp. 8-11); 

e) ways to draw closer links between adverse impacts to children and environmental harms and climate change in the text (pp. 11-12); 

f) barriers for children in accessing justice and effective remedies for corporate abuses and how the Directive can incorporate measures to overcome them (pp. 12-14); 

g)The additional supporting measures that will be required to enable the Directive to achieve its goals (pp. 15-16).

Save the Children and the International Commission of Jurists believe the proposed EU Corporate Sustainability Due Diligence Directive has the potential to have substantial impact on the conduct of businesses incorporated in and/or with operations in the European Union and the ability of these businesses to respect, protect and promote human rights and protect the environment, particularly if it embeds a more robust human rights and rights of the child approach. It is therefore important to assess how this proposed Directive complies with the State obligation to respect, protect, and fulfil human rights and fundamental freedoms, the corporate responsibility to respect and the need for rights and obligations to be matched to appropriate remedies. 

More specifically the directive could be strengthened by:
• Mainstreaming children’s rights in the Directive, including via explicit incorporation by reference to the CRC and relevant frameworks such as the Children’s Rights and Business Principles (CRBPs) in the preamble; 
• Expanding the scope of companies covered by the Directive to ensure alignment with the UNGPs and the UNCRC, as construed by the Committee in its CRC GC 16; 
• Ensuring that the Directive’s due diligence requirements covers the entire product cycle from raw materials to disposal/recycling, in line with the UNGPs; 
• Strengthening participation, transparency, and accountability by stakeholders, including children, where relevant, throughout the Directive; 
• Ensuring that the Directive requires company grievance processes to be fully accessible to children and child-sensitive, transparent in terms of procedure, type of complaints, number, and criteria, in compliance with international standards; and 
• Ensuring access to justice and legal remedies are fully accessible to children and are child-sensitive, in compliance with international standards.

Please find more details regarding our recommendations in the attached position paper.

A robust intellectual property framework is the essential key to promote innovation and to drive investments towards innovative manufacturing. The COVID19 crisis demonstrates that the current IP system has successfully played a crucial role in enabling innovation and collaboration, between biopharmaceutical innovators, Governments and research partners to speed up unmet medical needs, including hundreds of potential COVID19 treatments, diagnostics and vaccines. 
Over 13 bln doses of COVID19 vaccines have been produced, with voluntary technology transfer playing a significant role; there are approximately 376 collaborations for vaccine development, manufacture and distribution, 88% of which involve technology transfer and approximately 191 collaborations for therapeutics development, manufacture and distribution (https://www.efpia.eu/media/636693/getting-the-world-vaccinated-against-covid19.pdf).
Current international/EU context and Member States laws, do not require any further intervention to regulate national/international IP frameworks (including compulsory licensing – “CL”). They already have proper rules&practices, including flexibilities as provided by TRIPs, to permit customized solutions to real-world problems that may arise in any circumstances, including public health crisis.
Farmindustria, in line with EFPIA position, underlines that the hundreds of collaborations needed to produce novel treatments have been enabled by IP.
All of this work took place at pace without the need to resort to CL. Instruments such as CL should remain an exceptional, last-resort measure, applicable only in case of failure of voluntary agreements, as the Commission says in its Call for Evidence. 
Collaborations should be facilitated and encouraged, not undermined by inappropriate use of CL, that would create uncertainty for future investment and reduces incentives
Therefore, the baseline scenario should never be rejected without a clear impact assessment by considering all relevant factors. 
Any action at EU level – of which, actually, no need is envisaged also in the call of evidence itself - must comply anyway with TRIPs (including Art 31) and at least should take into account the following:
1.	nature of the “crisis” must be clearly and rationally defined, as well as who will decide whether and where it exists and when it ends;  
2.	activation of the HERA emergency framework as a necessary but not sufficient condition of any EU action on CL in the healthcare space;  
3.	CL should only be available if it is the ultimate resource to enable timely supply of sufficient quantities of products needed to address the specific crisis and if the licensee can supply required quantities to the required quality standard, and who should decide that;
4.	the patentee should be involved at all stages in the process; it must be demonstrated that voluntary agreements have failed without specific reasons and within a reasonable time given the context.  Further the patentee should be able to make representations as to whether the CL is needed according #3;
5.	geographical scope and duration of the CL which should reflect #1;
6.	CL should not constitute a means to get free access to valuable technology or to facilitate misappropriation of patented technology and/or related know-how;
7.	compensation payable under the CL;
8.	a procedure to enable swift challenge to any decision taken under #3-6.   
The commitment given by European Communities&Member States not to use the system established by TRIPs Art 31 bis as importing Members must be adhered to.
Lastly, as for the EU Regulation 816/2006, no evidence is given also in this case that there are problems with it, either because patents do not represent a barrier to access and therefore CLs are not a solution (other voluntary measures are more efficient for increasing supply of patented products), or because there is no demand for exports under CL from the EU due to price competition.

Observamos que se plantea una ampliación de las tareas de fabricante y organismos notificados, especialmente en el sistema 2+ que es que siguen todos los elementos prefabricados de hormigón estructural con marcado CE obligatorio. Para visualizar la diferencia entre lo que hay con la versión de 2013 y lo que este borrador pretende imponer:

RPC 2013	El organismo de certificación del control de producción notificado emitirá el certificado de conformidad del control de producción en fábrica sobre la base de: 
i) la inspección inicial de la planta de producción y del control de producción en fábrica, 
ii) la vigilancia, evaluación y supervisión permanentes del control de producción en fábrica.

Borrador RPC 2022	El organismo notificado emitirá el certificado de conformidad del control de la producción en fábrica sobre la base de:
i) la confirmación de que se han determinado correctamente el tipo de producto y la categoría de productos y la confirmación de que se han evaluado correctamente las prestaciones del producto sobre la base del examen de la documentación del producto;
ii) la inspección inicial de la planta de producción y del control de la producción en fábrica;
iii) la verificación completa de las tareas de la letra a), incisos iv) y v).
i) una evaluación de las prestaciones del producto sobre la base de ensayos (incluido el muestreo de los artículos que deban considerarse representativos del tipo), cálculos de tipo, valores tabulados o documentación descriptiva del producto;
ii) el control de la producción en fábrica;
iii) ensayos de muestras tomadas en la fábrica, de acuerdo con el plan de ensayos prescrito;
iv) la verificación de si la documentación técnica demuestra plenamente la correcta aplicación del presente Reglamento en lo que respecta a la evaluación de las prestaciones;
v) la verificación de si la documentación técnica demuestra plenamente la conformidad con los requisitos de producto del presente Reglamento.

En una primera lectura, se amplía aparentemente la carga de evaluación por parte del organismo certificador, especialmente en lo que se refiere a los dos últimos puntos que son una evaluación técnica, más o menos profunda, algo que a falta de más detalles de cómo se materializaría (no sé si en un futuro acto delegado) iría mucho más allá de la labor que habitualmente se ha venido desempeñando que era analizar que la producción se controlaba adecuadamente y conforme a la/s norma/s aplicables. Esto puede ser muy complejo de aplicar, ya que pasamos de un organismo externo certificador que evaluaba el control de producción en fábrica, a uno que además evalúa aspectos técnicos (difícil prever cómo se desarrollaría este punto).

The following is only an extract.
See the attached file for full response.
Best regards.

FIVRA fully subscribes to the necessity, as identified by the European Commission, to revise, repair and complete the EU Construction Products Regulation (CPR, EU 305/2011). We share the spirit, intentions and objectives of the overall Commission proposal as many of its specific constituents largely reflect the subjects / areas of attention put forward by Eurima (the European Association we belong to) in its contribution to the initial CPR-consultation process in 2021.
At the same time, we need to acknowledge that the proposal overall remains complex and requires the necessary technical knowledge and experience to be fully assessed and understood while a number of specific elements still require further clarification. This additional consultation and the intermediate Q&A- process initiated by DG GROW prior to the further legislative procedure in Council and Parliament are helpful in this process.
The enlarged subject matter and scope of the proposal combined with the need to ensure better / simpler regulation, legal certainty and continuity between the current CPR, the transitional CPR Acquis-process and the future revision, require common understanding, continuous exchange and cooperation, good governance and continuous impact assessment ahead of and throughout the further legislative-process and subsequent implementation of the proposal.
This contribution to the European Commission consultation aims to express support but also to identify areas and points of concern / attention / clarification as input into this ongoing process.

The attached file in summary:
- Full support to revise, repair and complete the CPR;
- The CPR is an essential instrument for Green Deal and Green/Digital transition delivery;
- The proposal is complex and technical and needs to be coherent and consistent at all times with the surrounding EU regulatory framework to avoid overlaps and “double-regulation”;
- Standardization / technical specifications must remain the root of the CPR;
- Standardisation process needs to be improved with early involvement of EC;
- Increased use of Delegated Acts is good fall back but needs due process and transparency;
- Need for continuity between current CPR – CPR Acquis process and revised CPR;
- Support for inclusion of Sustainability / Circularity and “codifying” EPD’s and EN15804;
- Need for centralised databases to accommodate digitalisation / BIM;
- Harmonized zone is essential to avoid mushrooming of national systems / requirements;
- Reinforced Market surveillance through more stringent 3rd party AVCP system is needed;
- Reducing administrative burdens / simplified procedures should not mean less compliance;
- Specific areas / points for clarification (Annex).

A seguito di un'attenta lettura della proposta di Regolamento, si riscontra che alcune delle proposte non siano condivisibili. Di seguito si cercherà di commentare succintamente gli art. 2 e 4 ed i principali temi che non trovano il nostro favore:
- Art. 2:  sarebbe consigliabile definire l’ambito di applicazione dopo la fine dei lavori del CPR Acquis, il che darebbe una idea più chiara dell’ambito di applicazione del Regolamento dei prodotti da costruzione. I punti dall’ (a) al (g)  richiamati nell’articolo  2, non risultano essere un elenco esaustivo, poiché in alcuni casi vengono richiamati elementi puntuali, mentre in altri si generalizza i prodotti. La marcatura CE ed il rispetto delle norme armonizzate ha portato ad un innalzamento della qualità dei prodotti da costruzione, nei paesi membri, giacchè i produttori sono tenuti a rispettare i requisiti imposti dalle norme armonizzate, che normalmente vengono pubblicate cercando di mantenere un condivisibile buon livello di qualità dei prodotti, perciò è molto importante una chiara e completa definizione dei prodotti.
- Art. 4:- “Caratteristiche essenziali dei prodotti” non è chiaro il passaggio dei precedenti requisiti essenziali a quelli della presente proposta, bisognerebbe approfondire in tavoli specializzati l’argomento prima di pubblicare una nuova versione del regolamento;
Considerazioni generali:
-Innanzitutto, la EC dovrebbe porre molta attenzione nell'aggiornare con continuità i mandati che permettano un veloce aggiornamento delle normative in accordo al CPR, in sostituzione alla proposta di realizzare atti delegati per la esecuzione di Specificazioni tecniche delegate;
-Dal momento che esiste una norma armonizzata, il prodotto dovrebbe essere messo in commercio soltanto nel rispetto della norma armonizzata con la apposizione della marcatura CE;
-La marcatura CE non dovrebbe essere mai volontaria tranne nei casi dove non esiste una norma armonizzata;
-Si condivide la possibilità di apporre altre marcature secondo quanto indicato nell' art. 18, ma gli stati membri non possono indicare la obbligatorietà di queste marcature, ai prodotti, per essere immesse in commercio;
-. La EC deve fornire una solida base giuridica per il funzionamento del sistema CPR. Inoltre si segnala che gli attuali mandati rispondono alla Direttiva CPD e non al CPR, il che ha reso molto difficile l’aggiornamento delle normative. I mandati devono essere rivisti attraverso richieste di standardizzazione e devono essere fornite chiare indicazioni legali;
-Non è chiaro perché alcuni prodotti sarebbero “des-armonizzati”, questo potrebbe comportare grande confusione nel mercato senza essere sicuri di avere delle solide basi che portino a questa “des-armonizzazione”;
-Non si condivide la pubblicazione di norme “volontarie” o norme armonizzate volontarie, a seguito di atti delegati della EC per i prodotti da costruzione, giacchè potrebbe generare confusione nel mercato abbassando la qualità dei prodotti immessi, invece di innalzarlo;

CEN and CENELEC welcome the objective of the proposal to achieve a well-functioning single market for construction products, avoid overlap of EU legislation and contribute to the EU objectives of the green and digital transition. A summary of the CEN and CENELEC key messages is presented below. 

CEN and CENELEC:

• seek to work closely with the EC on the implementation of current CPR framework and the transition period and agree on an action plan to allow citation of some harmonized standards in the frame of the current CPR framework; 

• are willing to support the EC with developing guidance documents and supporting material for drafting of harmonized standards under the revised CPR (similar to the Joint Initiative on Standardization (JIS) Action 5);

• seek collaboration with the EC, in the context of the revised CPR, to describe the roles, responsibilities and timelines for the involved stakeholders during the standardization process from standardization requests through the drafting to citation; o seek to achieve closer alignment with the EC proposal for the revision of the CPR and EU Reg 1025/2012, acknowledging that CEN and CENELEC promote the principle that harmonized standards are voluntary; 

• propose that the EC set the specific product requirements directly in standardization requests; 

• request that harmonized standards are considered the primary route for the development of harmonized technical specifications in support of the CPR and the development of delegated acts should be exceptional and used in limited cases as a fallback solution instead of an alternative equal solution to harmonized standards; 

• request the EC to establish a flexible mechanism to update the list of essential characteristics whenever during the development of a harmonized standard under a standardization request new technological developments or specific need emerge;

• ask the EC to specify the exact products and intended uses (if needed) proposed to be excluded and request:
-that sanitary appliances are covered in the scope of the revised CPR 
-the EC to define the final scope in cooperation with member states, European Parliament and all relevant stakeholders. 

• seek a modification to Article 34(4), as presented in Annex 1, with regard to ”affordable price" to avoid misinterpretation; 

• call for the EC to consider EN 15804 as the reference method for the calculation of environmental performance of construction products and EN 15978 as the reference method for the calculation of environmental performance of construction works.

AFELMA fully subscribes to the necessity, as identified by the European Commission, to revise, repair and complete the EU Construction Products Regulation (CPR, EU 305/2011). We share the spirit, intentions and objectives of the overall Commission proposal as many of its specific constituents largely reflect the subjects / areas of attention put forward by our association in its contribution to the initial CPR-consultation process in 2021. 

At the same time, we need to acknowledge that the proposal overall remains complex and requires the necessary technical knowledge and experience to be fully assessed and understood while a number of specific elements still require further clarification. This additional consultation and the intermediate Q&A- process initiated by DG GROW prior to the further legislative procedure in Council and Parliament are helpful in this process. 

The enlarged subject matter and scope of the proposal combined with the need to ensure better / simpler regulation, legal certainty and continuity between the current CPR, the transitional CPR Acquis-process and the future revision, require common understanding, continuous exchange and cooperation, good governance and continuous impact assessment ahead of and throughout the further legislative-process and subsequent implementation of the proposal.

This contribution to the European Commission consultation aims to express support but also to identify areas and points of concern / attention / clarification as input into this ongoing process.

In summary:

- Full support to revise, repair and complete the CPR;
- The CPR is an essential instrument for Green Deal and Green/Digital transition delivery;
- The proposal is complex and technical and needs to be coherent and consistent at all times with the surrounding EU regulatory framework to avoid overlaps and “double-regulation”;
- Standardization / technical specifications must remain the root of the CPR;
- Standardisation process needs to be improved with early involvement of EC;
- Increased use of Delegated Acts is good fall back but needs due process and transparency;
- Need for continuity between current CPR – CPR Acquis process and revised CPR;
- Support for inclusion of Sustainability / Circularity and “codifying” EPD’s and EN15804;
- Need for centralised databases to accommodate digitalisation / BIM;
- Harmonized zone is essential to avoid mushrooming of national systems / requirements;
- Reinforced Market surveillance through more stringent 3rd party AVCP system is needed;
- Reducing administrative burdens / simplified procedures should not mean less compliance;
- Specific areas / points for clarification.

The attached document specifies these points in detail.

ELA position is to ensure that Lifts, escalators and moving walks and lifting appliances as well as their components are excluded from the scope of CPR.

In the current proposal, COM (2022) 144 final, the commission excludes only lifts and escalators plus components.

Scope 2 a): lifts subject to Directive 2014/33/EU of the European Parliament and of the Council, escalators and their components;

The ELA request is to extend that to cover moving walks and lifting appliances which are covered by the Machinery Directive, 2006/42/EC. 

The ELA proposal is therefore to change the 2 a) as:

Scope 2 a): lifts and their components subject to Directive 2014/33/EU of the European Parliament and of the Council; lifting appliances, escalators, moving walks, and their components subject to Directive 2006/42/EC of the European Parliament and the Council;

Le projet de révision du Règlement Produits de Construction a été publié fin mars 2022 par la Commission européenne, les négociations devant désormais s’étendre jusqu’en 2023 au moins. Le CLER – Réseau pour la transition énergétique propose sa contribution sur ce projet de texte, qui a le potentiel de contribuer à la réduction des impacts environnementaux tout au long du cycle de vie des bâtiments, dont les émissions carbone. 
Vous trouverez la contribution complète du CLER - Réseau pour la transition énergétique en PJ.

Si le CLER – Réseau pour la transition énergétique accueille favorablement plusieurs dispositions du projet de texte, l’association porte aussi plusieurs demandes :
- accompagner d’un plan de travail intégré au projet de texte la nouvelle possibilité de fixer des exigences environnementales sur les produits de construction ;
- supprimer les obstacles pour les produits à faible impact environnemental en agissant en priorité au niveau des caractéristiques intrinsèques des produits ;
- modifier en profondeur la gouvernance du règlement en confiant notamment les moyens adéquats à la Commission européenne d’avancer via des actes juridiques ;
- rendre obligatoire la diffusion de l’information sur la performance environnementale des produits tout au long de leur cycle de vie.

With the proposed reform of the EU Geographical Indications (GIs) system, presented on the 31st March 2022, the European Commission intends to pursue two general objectives, as stated in the Introductory Report to the proposal:
1)	strengthening the GIs’ protection system by ensuring effective protection of intellectual property rights in the Union, including harmonized and efficient registration procedures, in order to fairly reward producers for their efforts;
2)	increasing the adoption of GIs across the Union, for the benefit of the rural economy.

With these objectives in mind, Coldiretti would like to stress its appreciation and the importance of some of the Commission’s proposals, and in particular:
-	the harmonization of recognition requests, both from EU and non-EU countries, to simplify the bureaucratic procedure between the submission of an application and the registration, and encourage the participation of producers;
-	strengthening the protection of intellectual property rights and related controls on online sales platforms against fraudulent and deceptive registrations and practices, and counteracting the use of GIs in the domain name system, by generally improving the application of GIs rules on the internet;
-	greater inclusiveness in the action to combat the falsification of GIs, giving recognized producer associations the power to initiate actions with the customs authorities to prevent any harmful measures for the image of their product;
-	safeguarding the rights of GI producers and greater transparency towards consumers, by prohibiting the use of the GI’s name in the sales denomination of a processed product, if there is no agreement with the association of producers;
-	strengthening the role of producers, with the extension of the possibility of production planning to all sectors, as already provided for those of cheese and ham pursuant to EU Reg. 1308/2013.

However, Coldiretti would also like to underline that the Commission proposal also contains some critical aspects, on which it is necessary to intervene with requests for amendments, in order to make the GIs system more effective in terms of protection and enhancement, and more attractive for producers.

First, the provisions concerning the GIs in the wine sector are well defined in the current reference legislation (CMO) and this ensures consistency and adequacy to the specificities of this sector, which has never complained of inadequacies towards the Commission, if not for a certain slowness of the related EU procedures.

Furthermore, the revision of the legislation should set transparency objectives aimed at increasingly protecting the weakest links in the agrifood supply chain, namely farmers and consumers. In this regard, the obligation to indicate the name of the producer for private labels could be considered as transparency and protection action.

Finally, it will certainly be necessary to evaluate all the aspects of the Commission proposal in the light of the implementation of the new CAP provisions for the period 2023-2027, as some of them are not yet fully implemented.

Please, find a more detailed position on the proposed reform of the GIs system in the document attached.

Trasmettiamo in allegato la posizione del Consorzio per la Tutela del formaggio Pecorino Romano, in particolare i punti da migliorare  nella revisione del regime delle IG, a nostro parere, dovrebbero essere i seguenti: 

1)     Migliore valorizzazione del contesto nel quale operano i Gruppi;

2)     Regolamentazione ed armonizzazione della natura, struttura e funzioni dei Gruppi a livello UE;

3)     Identificazione di un’Agenzia Europea per la Qualità Agroalimentare alla quale affidare tutte le attività di gestione del riconoscimento e gestione delle IG, così come quella di rappresentare gli interessi dei Consorzi in tutti i contesti internazionali;

4)     Valorizzazione delle attività di finanziamento dei Gruppi non più limitata ai soli contributi degli Associati o contributi nazionali ed europei, bensì la possibilità di ricorrere a sistemi evoluti di finanziamento;

5)     Valorizzazione della reputazione delle IG e definizione di un quadro comune per il rilascio delle autorizzazioni all’uso e sfruttamento delle stesse come ingrediente in prodotti elaborati o trasformati.

The European Footwear Confederation (CEC) welcomes the main concepts of the EC proposal for a review of Eco-design Directive and their integration in the EU Strategy for Sustainable Textiles. We recognise the capacity of the proposal to ensure a sustainable future for our industry, a priority for our members, by establishing requirements and criteria for a greener production, limiting the proliferation of alleged green labels and schemes (art. 26.4b) in order to avoid greenwashing and prohibiting the destruction and disposal of unsold consumers products (art. 20). We particularly welcome the objective to create a level playing field for our companies vis-à-vis third-country actors, which often place products with different standards and certificates, and which safety is often put in question. An effective market surveillance and customs will remain essential and a priority. players will not be able to place products manufactured following different safety standards and tests. 
The CEC welcomes as well the introduction of the Digital Product Passport (arts 8-13): an increasing number of EU consumer are more conscious of the environmental challenges and opt for a greener lifestyle, basing their purchase decisions on sustainability information. A DPP with information on the environmental footprint of a product, its composition but also its origin, will enable consumers to buy more sustainably and support national and EU economies, while reducing the carbon footprint with their choices. The DPP would certainly allow the industry to improve transparency and product traceability, as well as fighting against counterfeited products. However, sufficient adaptation period should be provided to SMEs, and the general provisions on the support of SMEs included in art. 19 should be accompanied by well-defined actions at EU and local level allowing a proportionate effort for SMEs in the enforcement of the Regulation. 
Ecodesign will play a key role for the future of the footwear sector, albeit the concrete risk that the current diversity of designs and models, so much appreciated by worldwide consumers thanks to the assembly of multiple components and materials, will suffer from simplification by producing more uniform models and reducing their number. Furthermore, it is of utmost importance to exclude footwear from the first wave of products under the 2022 Ecodesign Regulation proposal due to the complexity of the product and manufacturing process. For the same reason, the “ecosystem approach” (i.e. equating textiles to footwear) shall be avoided: in addition to the already mentioned complexity of the product, the duration of life of a shoe and its materials is much longer that the one of textile or a clothing product. It is important that the durability of footwear, even more of leather footwear, is appropriately rewarded in the Ecodesign criteria. Their longer duration of service - which determines the product's environmental footprint - needs to be acknowledged appropriately.
Finally, any approach to ecodesign in footwear should also consider: 
•	The comfort of the footwear, prior to the stage of use;
•	The possibility of repairing and extending the useful life of footwear;
•	The recyclability potential depending on the composition/mixture of materials;
•	The possibility of having traceability of materials, to unify and have accessibility to databases of environmental impacts of materials and components used in footwear.
Concluding, the CEC urges the EC to take into account the specificities of footwear and the capability of SMEs before taking any decision related to ecodesign affecting the footwear sector, and to define, when necessary, smaller but achievable goals in order to make the green transition inclusive and effective.

Independent Retail Europe members believe that the new process to determine ecodesign requirements truly has the potential to change the way products will be consumed in future and that retail will play a pivotal role in this transition to greener products. Retailers’ in-store advisors and experts provide an essential added value to consumers, as they: 

1.	supply consumers with important information on the products, allowing them to make informed choices;
2.	create the all-important link to manufacturers to identify problems and remediate to them as efficiently as possible;
3.	provide additional services linked to the products, such as extended warranties and insurances; 
4.	supply innovative services such as repair services - including the printing of spare parts through 3D printing technologies - as well as renting services, the supply of second hand and refurbished products, etc. 

There is room for improvement of the proposal for an Ecodesign Regulation for it to be more efficient and thereby better achieve its objectives. First of all, the Regulation should recognise the added value of retailers in the transition to more sustainable consumption patterns and ensure that through the delegated acts the role of retailers is systematically integrated in the design phase, to anticipate their role in repair, refurbishment, or reuse. This will require manufacturers to share more information with retailers (for example for repair and the availability/provision of spare parts). Secondly, the role of each actor in the supply chain should be clear and where possible in line with the EU acquis with regard to operators’ responsibilities. Information requirements to the customer should remain the responsibility of manufacturers, who know their products best and can assess what is required for their safe and sustainable use. Retailers can only advise customers based on complete and accurate information provided by the manufacturer on the products. Finally, the right representation (labelling) of these information requirements will play a major role in nudging consumers towards opting for greener products and solutions: They will need to be transparent and concise, so as to give the customer the necessary information about the environmental performance of the product at first glance. 

More detailed suggestions for the improvement of the proposal are contained in the position paper attached

ABB welcomes the European Commission’s proposal for the draft Ecodesign for Sustainable Products Regulation (ESPR) launched as part of the broader Sustainable Product Initiative on 30 March 2022.

Building on the success of the existing EU Ecodesign framework, the proposal has the potential to substantially boost new circular business models across the EU, support further development of sustainable products and empower all actors across the value chain with the information necessary to take sustainability-driven decisions. As a market leader committed to having at least 80 percent of our products and solutions covered by our circularity approach by 2030, ABB would like to share its views and comments on the draft legislation. More specifically, ABB supports the core elements of the draft ESPR proposal, notably:

- Efforts to harmonize EU rules and reduce market fragmentation;
- Product-by-product approach for setting sustainability requirements for products;
- Economic incentives for circular markets, such as increased transparency, focus on green public procurement, etc.;
- Digital Product Passport as a tool to increase transparency across the value chain and ensure easier access to data and information for all stakeholders, empowering them to take sustainability-driven decisions;

In addition to the points above, ABB would also like to draw the Commission’s attention to several issues that require further clarification, notably: 

- Further clarity on regulation of Substances of Concern, especially the ESPR relationship with REACH, RoHS and other chemicals legislation;
- The governance, role and process of the Ecodesign Forum as a primary venue for consultations with external stakeholders;
- Alignment with harmonised industry standards;
- Third-party verification requirement;
- The protection of IPRs and other business information in the context of DPP, as well as further clarity on the DPP roles and responsibilities for market operators across the value chain.

Our input and recommendations are further elaborated in the attached document.

The European Infrared Heating Alliance (EIHA) supports the need to keep legislation updated and in line with the latest technological developments. In that light, we appreciate the European Commission’s proposals for an Ecodesign for Sustainable Products Regulation (ESPR). We believe that technological progress coupled with the current climate crisis necessitates measures that go beyond energy efficiency by including stringent sustainability, material, and resource efficiency requirements.

Nonetheless, we are concerned with certain elements that may bypass the potential offered by the ESPR as tool to achieve sustainability while simultaneously strengthening the European economy. As such, we recommend the following:
1. Align definitions with existing legislation: remove redundancies and avoid loopholes;
2. Further explanation needed for the Digital Products Passport;
3. Ensure that substances of concern are selected by experts on the subject;
4. Improve the labelling requirements by making them streamlined and more sustainable;
5. Avoid disclosing of skewed competitively sensitive information on unsold goods;
6. Remove data duplication to support market surveillance;
7. Support for anti-circumvention clauses;
8. Include customs in the surveillance system to support market surveillance;
9. Ensure that standards are being followed, and not the other way round to avoid loss of expertise and inconsistencies; and
10. Avoid one-size-fits-all and maintain specific considerations for ErP groups. 

Our recommendations are further elaborated in the enclosed joint industry position paper.

